.TH EVENT_LOOP_REG_GENERATION 3 "2026-07-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_reg_generation \- caller-visible identity token for a registration's fd
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

uint64_t event_loop_reg_generation(const event_reg *reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a monotonically increasing value, unique loop-wide, minted once
when the fd (or queue/channel bridge) reg belongs to is first
registered (event_loop_add()'s new-entry path) and shared by every
registration on that same fd for as long as it lives; including
across event_loop_modify() (a direction flip is the same underlying
fd/connection, so it keeps the same generation) and across both a read
and a write registration on the same fd (both share one generation,
since they represent one logical connection).

This exists for a caller's own defensive bookkeeping across fd reuse, a
direct analogue of what facil.io's uuid gave callers in this library's
own HTTP modules: a caller holding onto a connection object across
several asynchronous steps can stamp it with the generation it read
right after event_loop_add() returned, and later compare against a
fresh read to detect whether it is still reasoning about the same
logical connection, or whether the underlying fd number has since been
closed and reused by an unrelated new connection.

Generation is not required for basic correctness. event_loop's own
dispatch path already validates a registration's liveness before
invoking any callback, unconditionally, whether or not a caller ever
calls this function at all: with more than one reactor thread, epoll's
own level-triggered semantics can hand the same still-ready entry to
more than one thread's concurrent epoll_wait(2) call (the classic
"thundering herd"), and a registration removed (or whose fd was reused
by a brand-new registration) between one such batch being fetched and
processed is safely skipped at dispatch time rather than misdispatched
or leading to a use-after-free.  This function's return value is purely
for the calling application's own bookkeeping on top of that guarantee.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
reg  Registration to query.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the generation value.
Returns 0 if reg is NULL (0 is never a valid generation for a real
registration; the counter starts at 1).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call even on an already-removed reg: like event_loop_modify()'s
own safe handling of a stale reg pointer, this reads a field set once
at registration and never written again, protected by reg's own
deferred-free contract rather than requiring reg to still be live.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, at any time, including concurrently with
dispatch, event_loop_modify(), and event_loop_remove().
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct conn {
    event_reg *reg;
    uint64_t generation;
} conn_t;

conn_t *c = malloc(sizeof(*c));
c->reg = event_loop_add(loop, selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                        handlers, c, NULL);
c->generation = event_loop_reg_generation(c->reg);

/* ... later, from some other asynchronous step holding a conn_t* ... */
if (event_loop_reg_generation(c->reg) != c->generation) {
    /* stale: this fd number has been closed and reused since */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_add (3),
.BR event_loop_modify (3),
.BR event_loop_remove (3)
